# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Crate 42 Misses the Downbeat

**Story & Context:** At 128 BPM, Tiana opens with a four-fragment Cross-String Relay: low-course tonic-fifth, high-course minor-third turn, low-course octave, high-course neighbor-note answer. Keon assigns kick and short bass to the low fragments, clap and hat to the high fragments. On the third cycle Tiana crosses one sixteenth early; Bruk Hinge removes the expected downbeat kick and delays the bass until the offbeat. The missing kick becomes the hook. Miora enters only after the high-course answer with one muted kabosy fifth. Noah marks every completed Interlock Debt cycle with a single woodblock. Lova appears in the middle section when the analog bass drops out, answering the final low-course note with one short bass-saxophone fall.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Export crate stamped 42 beside a printed rhythm grid with the first downbeat square left empty, a valiha string diagram crossing diagonally over a mango grading label, rainwater streaking the warehouse floor. Sparse lyric fragments: “crate forty-two,” “miss the one,” “cross above,” “answer below.”

## Track 2: Upper Course Waits Through the Rain

**Story & Context:** At 132 BPM, the track begins with two consecutive low-course valiha fragments and no high-course reply. Interlock Debt therefore forces the next section to begin with high course alone. Keon removes kick and bass for four bars, leaving clipped clap, short hats, handclaps, and upper valiha. Miora uses Course Shadow to hold one dry kabosy dyad from the abandoned low register. Palm Vacancy appears after every third upper-course accent, creating tiny holes where only bamboo decay and warehouse rain remain. When the low course finally returns, the full broken-beat grid reappears without a fill.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Rain-soaked grading window with two horizontal ledgers labeled LOW and HIGH, the HIGH row left blank for several entries before receiving a dark graphite mark, wet bamboo strips resting beside a red export stamp. Sparse lyric fragments: “upper course waits,” “rain on tin,” “debt comes back,” “leave the gap.”

## Track 3: Both Hands Leave the Sorting Table

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 118 BPM and begins with the sparsest groove on the EP: one kick every bar, low/high valiha fragments, and occasional handclaps. Tiana gradually shortens the distance between course changes until Cross-String Relay becomes an almost continuous braid. Instead of increasing drum density, Keon introduces more Palm Vacancies, so the faster hand motion produces a more perforated electronic grid. Miora plays only three kabosy events in the first two minutes. Lova uses breath-only bass-saxophone attacks beneath the longest silent spaces. In the final minute, Tiana stops crossing and places both hands off the strings. The broken-beat machine continues for one bar, then Keon removes it piece by piece: hats, clap, bass, kick. The EP ends on the shared resonance of the bamboo cylinder and rain against the loading doors.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty fruit-sorting table after closing, a polished valiha resting across two wooden crates, two handprints in dust beside it, rain visible beyond the open loading bay, one unmarked grading card under a brass weight. Sparse lyric fragments: “both hands leave,” “sorting table,” “no next course,” “rain keeps count.”

